A||||6||JohnnyK||Building a 6-pack cooler around a/c condenser||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||06-20-2000||11:42 PM||||A friendly (I assume) neighbourhood Fiero man pulled into my driveway (my god he had a nice one) and told me of a trick that his buddy did.. He built a little box around the condenser (I think.. I would assume it would be the silver cannistery thing up front) to hold some beer or what not.. Is this possible, and would it cause any adverse effects? (I don't know, sounds like a cool little idea)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfixit58||06-21-2000||08:23 AM||||Actually, it's the little thingy the fan blows through to push cool air into the passenger's area. Sure, it may be possible. An A/C unit is just a refridgerator with you on the inside (see, the light does go off when you shut the door  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ). The only question is space. Did he build the box in the spair tire compartment or the floorboard fo the passenger side footwell?<P>Roy||||reg||4
Z||000002||JohnnyK||06-21-2000||11:09 AM||||In the spare tire place I think.. But will A/C still get inside? ||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfixit58||06-21-2000||11:32 AM||||It may. It will certainly reduce the efficiency of the A/C if the beverages are hot to begin with. As long as you don't interfere with the air flow to the passenger's compartment it should work fine.<P>How the A/C system works is the fan blows air (outside air if set to normal, re-circulated air if set to max) across the evaporator. The evaporator is cold so the air cools and then cools the compartment. <P>The evaporator's cooling ability is dependent on the system efficiency. At max charge, I'm not sure how cold the evaporator gets so it may take a LONG road trip to cool hot drinks.<P>Maybe someone with more A/C experience could enlighten us on the details.<P>Roy||||reg||2
Z||000004||wkayl||06-21-2000||03:36 PM||||Back in about 1973 we bought an old 1955 Chrysler at a police auction that had the roof cut out for a trap door for "road hunting".  It had a box under the hood with the A/C lines running through it. If I remember correctly it had the high pressure lines coiled around the inside of the box.  I don't know how good it worked because the engine was froze and we never got it running.||||reg||1
Z||000005||prof bobo||06-21-2000||04:16 PM||||"If memory serves me correctly . . ."<P>I saw someone cut a little 'igloo' sixpack cooler so as to fit around the A/C accumulator/dryer (yeah, that 'silvery shiny canister thingy' in the front compartment.  It does indeed get cold when the A/C is running and would chill down your beveridges quite nicely.<P>Which I think is to what you're referring.<P>Can't think of any problems it might cause, unless you end up freezing your beers  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||hugh||06-21-2000||09:06 PM||||The air coming off your evaporator(the part that does the cooling)will at best be about 42 degrees.That is cold for you but not for your drinks.The only way you may be able to get any colder would be to isolate the air coming out of the evaporator so it will only cool your drinks.On a hot day your drinks will be cold and you will need them because you will probably be sweating your tail off. ||||reg||1
